Reset the query interval on regained reachability, not on every network signal.

Follow-up to the min-query-interval patch. That one resets the interval in
on_network_changed() so that arriving somewhere new is not delayed by up to
the interval. Measured over 5 hours of uptime on a laptop, the result ran at
roughly twice the configured rate: one query per 300 to 390 seconds against a
600 second interval.

on_network_changed() is not "the network changed". It is called from the
GNetworkMonitor "network-changed" signal, from the "notify::connectivity"
handler, and directly during construction, and on a link that flaps it fires
repeatedly for the same network. Every one of those was granting a free query.

Gate the reset on the rising edge of locate URL reachability instead. That is
the same edge locate_url_checked_cb() already computes and logs, and it is the
edge that actually means the service went away and came back: a suspend and
resume, or an association with a different network. A wobble that never takes
the locate URL out of reach no longer resets anything.

Also stop throttling a source that has nothing to replay. Throttling is only
tolerable because the consumer is handed the last response instead of nothing,
and priv->last_location is per-instance while the interval is process-global.
The sources are refcounted singletons destroyed when the last client drops, so
a consumer that starts a client after a quiet period can get a freshly
constructed source whose last_location is NULL, and would then wait out the
whole interval with no location at all rather than a slightly stale one. Let
that query through; it costs at most one query per source lifetime.

Known limitation: a move between networks where the locate URL never becomes
unreachable does not reset the interval, so such a move can be answered with
the previous location for up to the interval. get_internet_available() makes
that more likely, since it overrides an unreachable result whenever glib still
believes the internet is up. The bound is the interval and the consumer always
has a location, which is the trade this whole mechanism is making anyway.

--- a/src/gclue-web-source.c
+++ b/src/gclue-web-source.c
@@ -170,9 +170,15 @@
                 return;
         }
 
+        /* Throttling is only tolerable because the consumer gets the last
+         * response instead. A source with nothing to replay would starve it
+         * for the whole interval, so let the query through: the singletons
+         * are destroyed once the last client drops, and a consumer that
+         * returns after that would otherwise wait with no location at all.
+         */
         interval = gclue_config_get_min_query_interval
                 (gclue_config_get_singleton ());
-        if (interval > 0) {
+        if (interval > 0 && source->priv->last_location != NULL) {
                 gint64 now = g_get_monotonic_time ();
                 gint64 next = last_query_time +
                         (gint64) interval * G_USEC_PER_SEC;
@@ -368,6 +374,17 @@
                 g_debug ("Network changed: %s",
                          reachable ? "Enabling locate URL queries" :
                                      "Disabling locate URL queries");
+
+                /* Regaining the service is the one moment the replayed
+                 * answer is most likely wrong, so spend a query on it
+                 * immediately. Gated on the edge rather than on the
+                 * network-changed signal itself: GNetworkMonitor emits that
+                 * on every connectivity wobble, and on a flapping link most
+                 * of them are the same network, which was enough to run at
+                 * roughly twice the configured rate.
+                 */
+                if (reachable)
+                        last_query_time = 0;
         }
         if (!reachable)
                 return;
@@ -439,13 +456,6 @@
 
         cancellable_cancel_recreate (web);
 
-        /* A different network is the one moment where the cached answer is
-         * most likely wrong, so spend a query on it immediately. This is what
-         * keeps the throttle from stranding a machine that travelled while
-         * suspended on a location from the airport it left.
-         */
-        last_query_time = 0;
-
         if (web->priv->submit_url) {
                 submit_addr = g_network_address_parse_uri (web->priv->submit_url,
                                                            80, NULL);
--- a/data/geoclue.conf.in
+++ b/data/geoclue.conf.in
@@ -70,8 +70,8 @@
 # Minimum number of seconds between two queries to a web geolocation
 # service, across all sources. Queries that arrive sooner are answered with
 # the last known location, and a single query is scheduled for when the
-# interval expires. A change of network resets the interval, so arriving
-# somewhere new is not delayed.
+# interval expires. Regaining reachability of the service resets the
+# interval, so a resume or a move to another network is not delayed.
 #
